#
# D-136 — CONSERVE CHARACTERS, NOT HEADERS.
# D-139 — VERIFY EVERY BRANCH, NOT THE ONE THE FIXTURE EXERCISED.
#
# Both are the same shape: a check bound to the case the defect was reported on
# rather than derived over everything the input can be.
#
# D-136. GRIND-19 gave Foundry-Clear a conservation check -- it "may only
# destroy what it could account for" -- held by
# test_foundry_clear_refuses_a_broken_header_grammar_rather_than_truncating.
# That test passes ONLY because its fixture holds exactly ONE directive. The
# check compared HEADER COUNT to parsed-directive count, so it is blind to text
# the parser drops BEFORE the first recognised header: put one healthy
# directive beside the broken one and the counts reconcile.
#
# The first table is that driven on the ordinary live-run case -- one urgent +
# one normal, then the same '###' -> '##' hand-edit the shipped test itself
# performs. Row 2 is the defect: the counts rule CLEARS, and before this fix
# the urgent directive was GONE from directives.md while directives-cleared.md
# recorded only the normal one. D-129's filed harm word for word ("reports
# success, truncates the file, writes no archive record"), surviving the fix
# written to end it.
#
# The rule now SUBTRACTS instead of counting: the preamble, every line the
# parser reads as structure, and every directive body it actually parsed are
# each removed once from the file's text, and whatever still stands is text no
# archive record would carry. Bodies are subtracted longest-first so a
# directive that is a substring of another cannot consume the other's text and
# refuse a healthy file -- a trap the counting rule did not have, asserted as
# its own control.
#
# D-139. `_override_instruction` verified only its BARE branch and returned the
# quoted fallback UNVERIFIED. For the four class keys spelled like a wildcard
# that fallback did not merely fail, it read back as the WILDCARD:
#
#     'all'   -> rendered 'escalation-override: "all"'   -> read back {'*'}
#
# because `_override_value` stripped the surrounding quotes BEFORE the value
# was tested against `_OVERRIDE_ALL_VALUES` -- so quoting could not protect a
# class key spelled like a wildcard, which is the one thing quoting is for. The
# harm is not a no-op: an operator following the tool's OWN printed instruction
# to restore per-instance packets for the single class named 'all'
# de-escalated EVERY escalated class. Driven end to end with two escalated
# classes, AUTH_CONTRACT and all: structural=2 before, structural=0 after.
#
# Two fixes, one per half. The wildcard test now runs on the RAW quoting,
# before the quotes come off, so a bare `all` is the every-class spelling and a
# quoted `"all"` names the class. And every branch is verified: the candidate
# renderings are DERIVED from the quote characters the reader itself
# recognises, so a quote style the reader learns becomes a candidate the same
# day, and a key that survives none of them returns None rather than a marker
# the reader will act on differently. Both callers route through one renderer,
# so neither can interpolate that None into an instruction.
#
# The second table's ROWS are derived from `_OVERRIDE_ALL_VALUES` rather than
# typed, so a spelling added to the reader is a row here the same day. NFR-002
# is asserted beside it: the BARE form of every wildcard spelling still means
# every class.
